# 8. CAMPAIGN QUEUE AND RELIABILITY

Build the notification system so it continues working if the admin closes the browser.

Use Supabase PostgreSQL as the source of truth and a durable queue or equivalent persistent job-processing architecture.

Evaluate existing Supabase capabilities and current project conventions before selecting the implementation.

Use background workers, scheduled processing, and provider adapters as appropriate.

Each delivery job should include:

* Unique job ID.
* Campaign ID.
* Channel.
* Recipient or subscription reference.
* Template or message reference.
* Status.
* Attempt count.
* Next retry time.
* Provider message ID, when available.
* Last error.
* Created timestamp.
* Updated timestamp.

Suggested statuses:

* Pending.
* Processing.
* Accepted by provider.
* Sent, when supported.
* Delivered, when confirmed.
* Read, when supported.
* Failed.
* Retry scheduled.
* Cancelled.
* Skipped.

Use database constraints, idempotency keys, and atomic job claiming to prevent duplicate processing.

Use bounded exponential backoff with jitter for temporary errors.

Respect provider rate limits and retry-after information.

Do not retry permanent errors indefinitely.

Do not send duplicate notifications when a worker crashes after a provider has accepted a message.

Where a provider does not support idempotency, document and mitigate the uncertainty rather than promising exactly-once delivery.

Provide a campaign pause and cancel mechanism.

Do not cancel messages that have already been irreversibly accepted by a provider, but stop queued jobs that have not yet been sent.

